.TH dd "" "Command"
.SH NAME
dd \- file conversion and copying
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdd [\fIoption\fB=\fIvalue\fB] ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
dd -- file conversion
Usage:	dd [ option=value ] ...
Options:
	bs=n		Input and output block size
	cbs=n		Conversion buffer size
	conv=list	Comma-separated list of conversions:
		ascii	Convert ebcdic to ascii
		ebcdic	Convert ascii to standard ebcdic
		ibm	Convert ascii to ibm print codes
		lcase	Map all letters to lower case
		noerror	Continue on errors
		swab	Swap byte pairs
		sync	Pad input to 'ibs'
		ucase	Map all letters to upper case
	count=n		Number of blocks to copy from input
	files=n		Number of files to copy (useful with tape)
	ibs=n		Input buffer size
	if=file		Input file
	obs=n		Output block size
	of=file		Output file
	seek=n		Seek position of output
	skip=n		Number of input blocks to skip
.HE
.B dd
copies its input file to its output file, optionally performing one
or more conversions along the way, with input and output block sizes
that may differ from each other and from the buffering that
.BR cat (1)
or a shell redirection would use.
It is most useful for raw disk or tape devices and for exchanging
data with EBCDIC systems.
.PP
Every argument has the form
.IR option = value ;
there are no single-letter flags.
Any argument lacking an
.B =
or with nothing after the
.B =
is an error.
A numeric
.I value
may be followed by
.BR b ,
.BR w ,
or
.BR k ,
multiplying it by 512, by the size of an
.BR int ,
or by 1024, respectively; two such numbers separated by
.B x
are multiplied together.
All buffer sizes default to 512 bytes.
.IP \fBif=\fIfile\fR 0.9i
Read input from
.I file
instead of the standard input.
.IP \fBof=\fIfile\fR
Write output to
.I file
instead of the standard output.
.IP \fBibs=\fIn\fR
Set the input buffer size to
.I n
bytes.
.IP \fBobs=\fIn\fR
Set the output buffer size to
.I n
bytes.
.IP \fBbs=\fIn\fR
Set both the input and the output buffer size to
.I n
bytes; if
.B bs=
is the only sizing option given, input is copied to output without
the extra buffer that separate
.B ibs=
and
.B obs=
values require, which is faster.
.IP \fBcbs=\fIn\fR
Set the size of the intermediate conversion buffer used by the
.BR ascii ,
.BR ebcdic ,
and
.B ibm
conversions to
.I n
bytes (default: the input buffer size).
.IP \fBskip=\fIn\fR
Skip
.I n
input buffers (of size
.BR ibs )
before copying begins.
.IP \fBseek=\fIn\fR
Seek
.I n
output buffers (of size
.BR obs )
into the output file before the first write.
.IP \fBcount=\fIn\fR
Copy at most
.I n
input buffers.
.IP \fBfiles=\fIn\fR
Repeat the whole copy
.I n
times (for multi-file tape volumes); default 1.
.IP \fBconv=\fIlist\fR
Perform the comma-separated conversions named in
.IR list :
.RS
.IP \fBascii\fR 0.9i
Convert EBCDIC input to ASCII, trimming trailing blanks and appending
a newline to each converted record.
.IP \fBebcdic\fR
Convert ASCII input to standard (CACM) EBCDIC, blank-padding each
record to
.BR cbs .
.IP \fBibm\fR
Convert ASCII input to a variant EBCDIC table more suited to IBM
line-printer codes, blank-padding each record to
.BR cbs .
.IP \fBlcase\fR
Map upper-case letters to lower case.
.IP \fBucase\fR
Map lower-case letters to upper case.
.IP \fBswab\fR
Swap each pair of input bytes before writing.
.IP \fBsync\fR
Pad every input record short of
.B ibs
bytes with null bytes.
.IP \fBnoerror\fR
Continue with the next block after a read or write error instead of
stopping.
.RE
.PP
.B ascii
and
.BR ebcdic ,
.B ascii
and
.BR ibm ,
and
.B ucase
and
.B lcase
may not be combined with each other.
Any of the ASCII/EBCDIC conversions forces the use of the
intermediate conversion buffer, even if
.B ibs
equals
.BR obs .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
When it finishes,
.B dd
prints, on the standard error, the number of full and partial records
read and the number of full and partial records written, e.g.
.BR "20+1 records in" " and " "20+0 records out" .
It exits with status 1 if it was given a bad option, an out-of-memory
condition, or an unrecovered read/write error (one not masked by
.BR conv=noerror ).
.SH NOTES
Because implementations disagree on the mapping of some EBCDIC
graphic characters, such as braces and backslash, neither the
.B ebcdic
nor the
.B ibm
conversion table is right for every application; choose whichever one
matches the systems being interchanged with.
